     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against kotlinlang.org "Coroutine exceptions handling" (supervised children use the scope's
     CoroutineExceptionHandler like root coroutines; otherwise uncaught handling) and N. J. Smith, "Notes on structured
     concurrency, or: Go statement considered harmful" (2018), on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A user opens a profile, starts a 50 MB video download, and presses Back after two seconds. The screen is gone, but the download keeps running on a thread nobody holds. Ninety seconds later it finishes and tries to update a view that no longer exists. The crash report points at a line nobody would suspect.</p>

The download had no **owner**: nothing that ended when the screen ended. Structured concurrency gives every coroutine a parent and every parent a lifetime, so cancellation is requested down the owner's tree, and failures follow its parent/supervisor policy. Code still has to cooperate with cancellation. This chapter shows how the tree is built, how cancellation and failure move through it, and when supervision changes the rules.

<!-- @before Historical Context :: review -->
The next section says structured concurrency is "implemented in languages like Go". That sentence is under technical review. A correction is pending the owner's approval.

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: animation -->
### Cancellation down, failure up

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">One job tree under <code>viewModelScope</code></span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="node is-key" data-el="vm" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>viewModelScope</code> job</strong><small data-text="vmV">—</small></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="user" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>launch</code>: fetch user</strong><small data-text="userV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="posts" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>launch</code>: fetch posts</strong><small data-text="postsV">—</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="net" data-art="cloud" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>async</code>: network</strong><small data-text="netV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="db" data-art="database" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>async</code>: database</strong><small data-text="dbV">—</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Build the tree","caption":"Each builder creates a Job and attaches it as a child of the Job in the context it inherits. The tree mirrors the code's nesting.","add":["vm","user","posts","net","db"],"state":{"vm":"done","user":"active","posts":"active","net":"active","db":"active"},"text":{"vmV":"parent of both launches","userV":"running","postsV":"parent of two asyncs","netV":"running","dbV":"running"}},
{"label":"Failure goes up","caption":"The network call throws. In a regular (non-supervisor) hierarchy the failure cancels its parent: the scope in fetch posts that started both asyncs…","state":{"net":"error","posts":"warn"},"text":{"netV":"IOException","postsV":"failing"}},
{"label":"…and sideways","caption":"…which cancels its other child, the database read, so no result from a failed operation is half-delivered.","state":{"db":"ghost"},"text":{"dbV":"cancelled"}},
{"label":"Caught at a boundary","caption":"Here fetch posts started its asyncs inside coroutineScope, wrapped in try/catch for IOException. The scope rethrows the failure, the catch handles it, and it goes no further: the user fetch keeps running and the ViewModel job survives.","state":{"posts":"done","vm":"done","user":"active"},"text":{"postsV":"caught: shows an error state","userV":"still running"}},
{"label":"Owner cancelled","caption":"The user leaves; the ViewModel is cleared and viewModelScope is cancelled. Cancellation is requested for every remaining child. Cooperative suspension points or explicit checks let them stop; a blocking call or CPU loop that ignores cancellation can keep running.","state":{"vm":"ghost","user":"ghost","posts":"ghost"},"text":{"vmV":"cancelled","userV":"cancelled","postsV":"done"}}
]</script><figcaption>Cancellation is propagated down the job tree; the work must cooperate to finish. Unhandled child failures follow the parent/supervisor rules.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @diagram 129074e829ea6904 -->
### The tree in the Architecture section

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="node is-key"><strong>ViewModel job</strong><small>cancelled when the ViewModel is cleared</small></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong><code>launch</code>: fetch user</strong></div><div class="node"><strong><code>launch</code>: fetch posts</strong><small>parent of the two asyncs below</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node"><strong><code>async</code>: network</strong></div><div class="node"><strong><code>async</code>: database</strong></div></div></div><figcaption>If the ViewModel dies, its job is cancelled, and the cancellation cascades to every descendant.</figcaption></figure>

