<!-- @after Common Misconceptions :: check -->
**What happens next?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>Inside <code>coroutineScope { }</code>, the first of three <code>async</code> calls throws.</summary>

The other two are cancelled, and the scope rethrows the failure to its caller.
</details>

<details><summary><code>catch (e: Exception) { }</code> around a suspend call, and the screen closes mid-call.</summary>

The `CancellationException` is swallowed and the code after the `try` keeps running as if nothing happened. Rethrow cancellation, or catch only the exceptions you can handle.
</details>

<details><summary>A repository creates <code>CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.IO)</code> and launches a sync in it.</summary>

Nobody owns that work: it is not cancelled with any screen, and tests cannot wait for it. Expose a suspend function, or inject an application scope deliberately for process-level work.
</details>
